--TEST--
A `parse` declared `: static` answers the class the call named, so a subclass inheriting the member is built rather than the base that wrote it, and `Parses` is a type a value answers to — one parameter declared at the contract admits implementors sharing no ancestor — `rule:statements/static-is-a-member-modifier` and `rule:types/type-test`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Parses
// covers: lang:classes/parses
// What a class gains by claiming the contract is asserted here from both ends,
// because either end read alone looks like the whole of it.
//
// One end is what `parse` answers. It is declared `static`, so the class named at
// the call is the class that gets built -- a subclass inheriting the member is
// built rather than the base that wrote it, which a `parse` returning `self`
// could not have said. The other end is the contract as a type: a parameter
// declared at `Parses` admits any implementor, and two classes with no ancestor
// in common both arrive at one. That second end is what every route capture,
// query parameter and command argument declared at a class is a spelling of.
//
// `Parses` needs no `use`: it is one of the interfaces the compiler declares for
// every program, beside `Comparable` and `Stringable`.
class CouponCode implements Parses
{
    public tainted string $text = "";

    public function constructor(tainted string $text)
    {
        $this->text = $text;
    }

    public static function parse(tainted string $s): static
    {
        if ($s != Core\Str::upper($s)) {
            throw new ParseError("a coupon code is written in capitals");
        }
        return new static($s);
    }
}

// Declares nothing of its own, so the `parse` it answers through is the base's,
// unchanged. What it is built as is the whole question below.
class PromoCode extends CouponCode {}

// A second implementor, sharing no ancestor with the first. Without it the
// parameter below could be admitting one family rather than the contract.
class RoomNumber implements Parses
{
    public tainted string $text = "";

    public function constructor(tainted string $text)
    {
        $this->text = $text;
    }

    public static function parse(tainted string $s): static
    {
        if (Core\Str::length($s) == 0) {
            throw new ParseError("a room number is not empty");
        }
        return new static($s);
    }
}

final class Door
{
    // The binding site, with nothing around it: what is asked for is the
    // contract, never a list of the classes carrying it.
    public static function admits(Parses $value): string
    {
        if ($value is PromoCode) {
            return "a promo code";
        }
        if ($value is CouponCode) {
            return "a coupon code";
        }
        return "another implementor";
    }
}

// The base's own call answers the base, and is not its subclass.
var $base = CouponCode::parse("SUMMER24");
echo "base ", ($base is CouponCode) ? "yes" : "no", " ", ($base is PromoCode) ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// The subclass's call answers the subclass, through a member the subclass never
// declared -- and is still the base as well, which is what inheriting means.
var $sub = PromoCode::parse("WINTER10");
echo "sub ", ($sub is PromoCode) ? "yes" : "no", " ", ($sub is CouponCode) ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// Every implementor answers to the contract, including the one that reached it
// by extending another class rather than by naming it.
echo "is-parses ", ($base is Parses) ? "yes" : "no", " ", ($sub is Parses) ? "yes" : "no", " ", (RoomNumber::parse("204") is Parses) ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// Three classes, two of them unrelated, all admitted at one parameter -- and
// each still the class it was built as once it is through.
echo "admits ", Door::admits($base), "\n";
echo "admits ", Door::admits($sub), "\n";
echo "admits ", Door::admits(RoomNumber::parse("204")), "\n";

// A refusal is the class's own throw, and the binding site neither swallows it
// nor rewraps it: nothing gets past the door half-checked.
try {
    echo Door::admits(CouponCode::parse("summer24")), "\n";
} catch (ParseError $refusal) {
    echo "refused ", $refusal->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
base yes no
sub yes yes
is-parses yes yes yes
admits a coupon code
admits a promo code
admits another implementor
refused a coupon code is written in capitals
